WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.131 --> 00:00:03.384
[audience laughs]
[clock button clicking]

00:00:04.426 --> 00:00:07.054
Susan Polgar: If you
zoom in, you can see that

00:00:07.054 --> 00:00:09.598
in the corner there on
her right-hand side,

00:00:09.598 --> 00:00:11.142
it's a dark square,

00:00:11.142 --> 00:00:14.019
so that's when the board
is turned the wrong way.

00:00:14.019 --> 00:00:15.688
Hi, I'm Susan Polgar.

00:00:15.688 --> 00:00:18.858
I have won four World Chess Championships

00:00:18.858 --> 00:00:21.569
as well as five Olympic gold medals,

00:00:21.569 --> 00:00:24.822
and I have broken the
gender barrier in chess.

00:00:24.822 --> 00:00:26.365
Today, we are going to look at

00:00:26.365 --> 00:00:29.702
some video segments from movies

00:00:29.702 --> 00:00:32.288
where chess has been an important part.

00:00:39.378 --> 00:00:41.213
[audience applauds]

00:00:41.213 --> 00:00:43.299
Actually, this game that they are playing

00:00:43.299 --> 00:00:45.134
is based on a real game

00:00:45.134 --> 00:00:47.803
that was played by two grandmasters

00:00:47.803 --> 00:00:50.181
who happen to be close friends of mine,

00:00:50.181 --> 00:00:52.725
the Ukrainian Vassily Ivanchuk

00:00:52.725 --> 00:00:55.227
and the American Patrick Wolff.

00:00:55.227 --> 00:00:59.899
They played this game up
to this point back in 1993.

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:03.694
The fiction started in the
movie from a certain position,

00:01:03.694 --> 00:01:07.531
in a few moves that went
in a different direction.

00:01:07.531 --> 00:01:09.283
The actual game ended in a draw

00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:12.119
between those two grandmasters

00:01:12.119 --> 00:01:14.038
in, I think, 72 moves.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:18.417
The game is having a break.

00:01:18.417 --> 00:01:20.169
It's called "adjourned."

00:01:20.169 --> 00:01:23.464
Even in those days, it actually
gave an unfair advantage

00:01:23.464 --> 00:01:27.885
to the side who had better
coaches or friends or helpers

00:01:27.885 --> 00:01:31.347
to do the analysis of
that adjourned position.

00:01:31.347 --> 00:01:35.017
That option is no longer existing today.

00:01:37.645 --> 00:01:40.105
In real life, opponents don't usually

00:01:40.105 --> 00:01:41.732
stare at each other this much

00:01:41.732 --> 00:01:43.192
or look at each other.

00:01:43.192 --> 00:01:45.194
To some degree they look a little bit,

00:01:45.194 --> 00:01:47.279
but they are just usually more busy

00:01:47.279 --> 00:01:51.742
in trying to figure out the
best moves and calculations.

00:01:52.993 --> 00:01:55.955
Chess players, some look
at the ceiling, some don't.

00:01:55.955 --> 00:01:58.707
But regardless, that's pretty much

00:01:58.707 --> 00:02:01.877
what is happening when
we calculate a variation.

00:02:01.877 --> 00:02:03.379
We see the pieces moving.

00:02:03.379 --> 00:02:05.839
Even though we have a
certain specific position

00:02:05.839 --> 00:02:09.468
right in front of us, we need to foresee

00:02:09.468 --> 00:02:12.137
when the pieces move
one, two, three, five,

00:02:12.137 --> 00:02:14.265
10, 20 moves ahead sometimes.

00:02:14.265 --> 00:02:16.809
In reality, some grandmasters do that,

00:02:16.809 --> 00:02:19.895
that they look away from the actual board,

00:02:19.895 --> 00:02:22.106
look at the ceiling or somewhere,

00:02:22.106 --> 00:02:26.360
just really almost in
a way that the position

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:28.654
that is physically
actually in front of them

00:02:28.654 --> 00:02:29.989
on the chessboard.

00:02:34.243 --> 00:02:35.703
She sacrificed the queen,

00:02:35.703 --> 00:02:37.037
which is very unusual,

00:02:37.037 --> 00:02:40.207
because it's the most valuable
piece we have in chess.

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:44.378
So, she got her queen back,

00:02:44.378 --> 00:02:46.505
because when a pawn
reaches all the way across,

00:02:46.505 --> 00:02:49.174
it can promote any piece,
including the queen.

00:02:49.174 --> 00:02:52.219
And so now she has a
major material advantage

00:02:52.219 --> 00:02:53.637
at this point in the game.

00:02:53.637 --> 00:02:55.681
The only thing she needs to worry about

00:02:55.681 --> 00:02:59.226
that if Borgov starts giving checks.

00:02:59.226 --> 00:03:00.811
And she blocked the check,

00:03:00.811 --> 00:03:02.604
the checkmates, an attack on the king.

00:03:02.604 --> 00:03:06.859
And now the black queen again
is attacking the white king.

00:03:06.859 --> 00:03:10.195
And now she finally found
a square for her king

00:03:10.195 --> 00:03:13.115
when there are no more dangerous checks.

00:03:13.115 --> 00:03:18.120
And, therefore, white's material
advantage is overwhelming,

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:20.497
and I think we'll see the
end of the game right here.

00:03:21.707 --> 00:03:23.459
[audience applauds]

00:03:23.459 --> 00:03:26.295
You know, I was a pioneer

00:03:26.295 --> 00:03:27.963
in a man's world of chess,

00:03:27.963 --> 00:03:31.467
and I didn't have such nice treatment.

00:03:31.467 --> 00:03:34.178
I was invited to a major elite tournament

00:03:34.178 --> 00:03:37.431
in Spain back in 1987,

00:03:37.431 --> 00:03:39.600
and one of the top players in the world

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.935
at the time who was one
of the participants,

00:03:41.935 --> 00:03:44.396
he objected that, "What are women doing

00:03:44.396 --> 00:03:46.648
in such a prestigious, elite tournament?

00:03:46.648 --> 00:03:48.942
Women shouldn't be invited."

00:03:48.942 --> 00:03:52.571
And another six-time US champion

00:03:52.571 --> 00:03:56.200
that I played actually a year earlier

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.993
swiped the pieces off the board.

00:03:57.993 --> 00:04:01.372
So this is kind of a little bit of

00:04:01.372 --> 00:04:04.958
a idealistic world and not the reality,

00:04:04.958 --> 00:04:07.836
not in the '70s or '80s or '90s,

00:04:07.836 --> 00:04:10.130
when I was actively playing,

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:13.926
and I think even less
so in the '50s or '60s.

00:04:13.926 --> 00:04:15.719
[chess pieces clacking]
[clock button clicking]

00:04:15.719 --> 00:04:18.138
So, they're playing
reasonable starting moves

00:04:18.138 --> 00:04:20.140
and normal opening moves.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.352
So, a good clock player
could do it, as well.

00:04:23.352 --> 00:04:25.938
Three games is a lot playing with a clock.

00:04:25.938 --> 00:04:27.523
I think they are playing five minutes,

00:04:27.523 --> 00:04:29.525
which is a traditional blitz game,

00:04:29.525 --> 00:04:31.360
five minutes for the entire game.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:34.196
Playing without the clock,

00:04:34.196 --> 00:04:38.742
I have played actually
326 games simultaneously,

00:04:38.742 --> 00:04:40.452
which was a world record at the time.

00:04:40.452 --> 00:04:43.455
So, three is not so much compared to that.

00:04:43.455 --> 00:04:45.165
[peppy music]

00:04:45.165 --> 00:04:46.291
[chess pieces clacking]
[clock button clicks]

00:04:46.291 --> 00:04:48.585
We're not seeing half of the chessboard

00:04:48.585 --> 00:04:49.837
and the actual moves

00:04:49.837 --> 00:04:51.547
to tell whether they are or not.

00:04:51.547 --> 00:04:53.048
When it comes to the moves,

00:04:53.048 --> 00:04:55.801
they are all definitely
possible positions,

00:04:55.801 --> 00:04:57.302
and there's nothing illegal.

00:04:57.302 --> 00:05:00.264
[peppy music]

00:05:01.390 --> 00:05:04.893
She was tipping over her opponents' kings

00:05:04.893 --> 00:05:07.229
in I think a few occasions.

00:05:07.229 --> 00:05:10.816
That's not something
that anybody would do.

00:05:11.984 --> 00:05:14.778
Additionally, historically,
tipping over the king

00:05:14.778 --> 00:05:17.322
used to be the sign of resigning the game.

00:05:17.322 --> 00:05:18.949
I definitely give thumbs up.

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:20.492
I really like this series,

00:05:20.492 --> 00:05:23.745
'cause it did really give
a huge boost for chess

00:05:23.745 --> 00:05:26.290
in general in the mainstream.

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:31.295
[suspenseful music]
[clock ticking]

00:05:32.463 --> 00:05:34.006
I actually really like this scene.

00:05:34.006 --> 00:05:36.175
It was one of my favorites, I think,

00:05:36.175 --> 00:05:38.844
and the position was very visible.

00:05:38.844 --> 00:05:40.471
You could see all the pieces.

00:05:40.471 --> 00:05:43.182
Attendant: Takes bishop.
[clock ticking]

00:05:44.641 --> 00:05:46.768
Susan: I remember when I was in Moscow

00:05:46.768 --> 00:05:48.896
and I've seen a tournament

00:05:48.896 --> 00:05:52.399
when I was still, like, 11, 12 years old.

00:05:52.399 --> 00:05:54.318
They were moving those big pieces

00:05:54.318 --> 00:05:56.987
on those big demonstration boards.

00:05:56.987 --> 00:06:00.824
And now we can view games
live through the internet,

00:06:00.824 --> 00:06:02.951
but back in the days, it was like this,

00:06:02.951 --> 00:06:05.913
that they had those so-called
demonstration boards

00:06:05.913 --> 00:06:09.249
to relay the actual
moves to larger crowds.

00:06:09.249 --> 00:06:12.586
There is a very special
thing ongoing on the board

00:06:12.586 --> 00:06:15.464
with this very last move
that we see right now,

00:06:15.464 --> 00:06:18.634
with the knight capturing the bishop.

00:06:18.634 --> 00:06:21.428
Attendant: King to rook two.

00:06:21.428 --> 00:06:22.679
[clock ticking]
[chess piece clacks]

00:06:22.679 --> 00:06:24.306
Susan: It is a winning position,

00:06:24.306 --> 00:06:26.433
because now the black
king is under attack,

00:06:26.433 --> 00:06:27.309
it's in check,

00:06:27.309 --> 00:06:30.562
and then the upcoming
moves that will follow up

00:06:30.562 --> 00:06:34.024
would lead to either major
material gain for white

00:06:34.024 --> 00:06:35.192
or checkmate.

00:06:37.069 --> 00:06:41.031
So, one of the things that
a lot of beginner players

00:06:41.031 --> 00:06:43.659
or non-chess players don't understand

00:06:43.659 --> 00:06:47.829
that why people resign until
the checkmate comes, right?

00:06:47.829 --> 00:06:51.542
And at the same time, it's
between experienced players,

00:06:51.542 --> 00:06:53.835
it's very normal that
they don't play the game

00:06:53.835 --> 00:06:55.295
all the way out.

00:06:55.295 --> 00:06:57.839
When they see that checkmate is coming

00:06:57.839 --> 00:07:01.593
in two, three, four, five
moves, or even more than that,

00:07:01.593 --> 00:07:03.011
or they lose their queen,

00:07:03.011 --> 00:07:06.348
which is one of the most
important pieces, you know,

00:07:06.348 --> 00:07:10.811
they would give the respect
to the opponent that,

00:07:10.811 --> 00:07:12.771
"OK, I know what's coming."

00:07:12.771 --> 00:07:14.481
MacAdams: My congratulations,
sir. A brilliant coup.

00:07:14.481 --> 00:07:16.608
Susan: Applauding is not so much

00:07:16.608 --> 00:07:18.193
part of the chess scene.

00:07:18.193 --> 00:07:20.028
It could be because the game itself

00:07:20.028 --> 00:07:22.030
was so aesthetically pleasing,

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:23.365
it's just something unusual.

00:07:23.365 --> 00:07:26.159
You did something funny
or tricky, or, you know,

00:07:26.159 --> 00:07:28.120
and that's what happened
a little bit here.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:31.999
So they were enjoying it as
an art, as a performance.

00:07:31.999 --> 00:07:34.585
Chess-wise, it was also one of the best.

00:07:34.585 --> 00:07:37.212
I definitely would give a 10.

00:07:37.212 --> 00:07:39.172
Ron: You there, d5!

00:07:39.172 --> 00:07:43.802
[stone rolling]
[dramatic music]

00:07:43.802 --> 00:07:46.513
[blades unsheathe]
[stone crumbles]

00:07:46.513 --> 00:07:48.473
Susan: So, those were really
normal starting moves.

00:07:48.473 --> 00:07:52.394
It's called the center counter
or the Scandinavian defense.

00:07:52.394 --> 00:07:54.396
[stone exploding]

00:07:57.190 --> 00:07:59.818
But then I think they were
more focused on the visuals

00:07:59.818 --> 00:08:03.113
of pieces getting captured and eliminated.

00:08:03.113 --> 00:08:05.157
The first three moves actually

00:08:05.157 --> 00:08:06.783
that they did play on the board,

00:08:06.783 --> 00:08:10.037
they are very clear and very realistic.

00:08:10.037 --> 00:08:11.997
They didn't show even the whole board,

00:08:11.997 --> 00:08:15.208
and it's very hard to make out
what's going on after that.

00:08:18.045 --> 00:08:20.339
Tobey's a great actor and everything,

00:08:20.339 --> 00:08:21.923
but just the pure fact

00:08:21.923 --> 00:08:26.178
that he's just so much smaller in size

00:08:26.178 --> 00:08:28.805
than his opponent here, Spassky.

00:08:28.805 --> 00:08:30.641
The real Bobby Fischer,

00:08:30.641 --> 00:08:33.352
who has been a friend
of mine and I knew well,

00:08:33.352 --> 00:08:36.938
is a very tall, big guy

00:08:36.938 --> 00:08:38.565
and humongous hands.

00:08:38.565 --> 00:08:39.775
Commentator: Fischer has abandoned

00:08:39.775 --> 00:08:41.818
his trademark Sicilian opening.

00:08:41.818 --> 00:08:44.112
Susan: Talk about Fischer

00:08:44.112 --> 00:08:47.115
abandoning his favorite
Sicilian opening --

00:08:47.115 --> 00:08:50.160
the Sicilian is not white's choice.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:52.746
I mean, Bobby Fischer used to be known

00:08:52.746 --> 00:08:55.374
to start almost all his games

00:08:55.374 --> 00:08:58.669
with his king pawn to e4,

00:08:58.669 --> 00:09:00.754
and then moving the pawn up two squares

00:09:00.754 --> 00:09:02.589
in front of his king.

00:09:02.589 --> 00:09:04.758
That allows the Sicilian,

00:09:04.758 --> 00:09:07.552
but Sicilian is a choice of black,

00:09:07.552 --> 00:09:08.720
not of white.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:11.223
So that's a little bit
inaccurate, I would say,

00:09:11.223 --> 00:09:15.018
when it comes to phrasing it that way,

00:09:15.018 --> 00:09:17.187
that he "abandoned the Sicilian."

00:09:17.187 --> 00:09:19.731
He abandoned allowing the Sicilian,

00:09:19.731 --> 00:09:21.608
but he didn't abandon the Sicilian.

00:09:21.608 --> 00:09:24.194
[dramatic music]
[clock button clicks]

00:09:27.864 --> 00:09:31.076
Bobby used to be known
to play an e4 player,

00:09:31.076 --> 00:09:32.744
which means starting the game

00:09:32.744 --> 00:09:36.039
with moving the pawn up
in front of his king.

00:09:36.039 --> 00:09:38.875
And in this game that's
being shown in this segment

00:09:38.875 --> 00:09:42.129
as well as in the real
game six of the match,

00:09:42.129 --> 00:09:44.339
Fischer started with c4,

00:09:44.339 --> 00:09:48.176
moving the pawn in front
of his queen-side bishop.

00:09:48.176 --> 00:09:51.138
And that's the first
time he'd ever done that.

00:09:51.138 --> 00:09:53.348
And that actually is really important,

00:09:53.348 --> 00:09:55.809
because the element of surprise

00:09:55.809 --> 00:09:58.228
is quite important in chess,

00:09:58.228 --> 00:10:01.022
and that's what he was
shooting for in this game.

00:10:01.022 --> 00:10:02.941
And, actually, I learned from it,

00:10:02.941 --> 00:10:05.318
and when I played my world championship

00:10:05.318 --> 00:10:08.530
against the Chinese champion Xie Jun,

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:10.741
I also did that similar trick

00:10:10.741 --> 00:10:14.703
in that I was known to
usually start with d4,

00:10:14.703 --> 00:10:16.705
with the pawn in front of my queen.

00:10:16.705 --> 00:10:21.293
And that helped me win my last,
fourth world championship.

00:10:21.293 --> 00:10:23.920
I also started with a different move,

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:26.757
moving my pawn in front of my king.

00:10:26.757 --> 00:10:28.842
[Spassky applauds]

00:10:28.842 --> 00:10:30.677
So, this is very unusual,

00:10:30.677 --> 00:10:35.265
that somebody would smile and be so nice.

00:10:35.265 --> 00:10:36.725
But I know Boris Spassky,

00:10:36.725 --> 00:10:39.311
he's been a friend of
mine for many, many years,

00:10:39.311 --> 00:10:40.896
and, actually,

00:10:40.896 --> 00:10:44.232
that's what he did at
the actual championship.

00:10:44.232 --> 00:10:47.235
But it's very, very uncommon.

00:10:47.235 --> 00:10:49.571
Usually people are closer to crying

00:10:49.571 --> 00:10:52.282
and holding their tears back than smiling.

00:10:52.282 --> 00:10:54.451
Announcer: This is the final round

00:10:54.451 --> 00:10:57.579
of our Rwabushenyi National
Chess Championship.

00:10:57.579 --> 00:11:00.499
Phiona Mutesi and Christine Namaganda

00:11:00.499 --> 00:11:03.376
are tied for first position
in the women's category.

00:11:03.376 --> 00:11:05.879
They will now play the
final deciding game.

00:11:05.879 --> 00:11:07.130
Susan: Everything is correct.

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:08.965
We can see the first few moves.

00:11:08.965 --> 00:11:11.384
That's the queen's gambit, coincidentally,

00:11:11.384 --> 00:11:13.970
where black has a choice
to accept the gambit.

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:16.807
That would mean to capture the pawn on c4

00:11:16.807 --> 00:11:19.518
or play the declined.

00:11:19.518 --> 00:11:23.355
You see the focus and the
concentration of the players here.

00:11:23.355 --> 00:11:26.650
Phiona, who is actually a real person.

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:28.819
Katende: You belong here!

00:11:28.819 --> 00:11:30.320
Administrator: Silent.
Susan: You're not supposed to

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:32.113
just speak loudly and disturb

00:11:32.113 --> 00:11:34.449
the concentration of the players.

00:11:34.449 --> 00:11:36.243
Well, once in a while it happens,

00:11:36.243 --> 00:11:37.953
but it's very, very rare,

00:11:37.953 --> 00:11:40.914
and usually that person is being

00:11:40.914 --> 00:11:43.792
escorted out immediately
when that may happen.

00:11:44.918 --> 00:11:47.212
I mean, there has been
some extreme situation

00:11:47.212 --> 00:11:49.756
when somebody got really sick or even died

00:11:49.756 --> 00:11:51.675
during the chess tournament.

00:11:51.675 --> 00:11:55.053
But other than that, it's just not normal

00:11:55.053 --> 00:11:56.471
that people would speak.

00:11:56.471 --> 00:11:57.639
[dramatic music]

00:11:57.639 --> 00:11:58.765
[clock button clicks]

00:12:01.893 --> 00:12:04.312
I managed to figure
out the exact positions

00:12:04.312 --> 00:12:06.356
and then what was going on.

00:12:06.356 --> 00:12:09.317
So I would definitely
give a very high mark

00:12:09.317 --> 00:12:12.863
for the technical
expertise of the directors

00:12:12.863 --> 00:12:15.365
and the chess advisors here,

00:12:15.365 --> 00:12:16.616
because they did a good job

00:12:16.616 --> 00:12:20.370
in very realistically showing

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:22.747
the actual chess positions.

00:12:22.747 --> 00:12:24.791
[chess pieces clacking]
[clock button clicking]

00:12:27.752 --> 00:12:28.879
And as you can see,

00:12:28.879 --> 00:12:31.089
the pawn got all the way to the other end.

00:12:31.089 --> 00:12:32.883
And you can see that whole sequence,

00:12:32.883 --> 00:12:34.676
that they make a move,
they press the clock,

00:12:34.676 --> 00:12:36.136
they write the move down.

00:12:36.136 --> 00:12:38.346
So that's also very correct.

00:12:38.346 --> 00:12:41.766
I would give this segment definitely a 10.

00:12:41.766 --> 00:12:45.312
Magneto: That foolish
law, or one just like it,

00:12:45.312 --> 00:12:47.022
and come for you?

00:12:47.022 --> 00:12:49.733
Susan: Not as practical
to see through the board

00:12:49.733 --> 00:12:52.110
to differentiate even the colors,

00:12:52.110 --> 00:12:54.696
which one is the white,
which one is the black,

00:12:54.696 --> 00:12:57.324
but it's obviously visually very pleasing.

00:12:57.324 --> 00:12:59.117
Magneto: And your children?

00:12:59.117 --> 00:13:02.787
[dramatic music]
[chess piece clacks]

00:13:02.787 --> 00:13:04.623
Charles: It does indeed.

00:13:04.623 --> 00:13:06.958
Susan: There, I mean,
here they can trash-talk

00:13:06.958 --> 00:13:08.543
or psych each other out,

00:13:08.543 --> 00:13:12.672
which obviously is normal in casual games

00:13:12.672 --> 00:13:15.842
or coffeehouse chess or in parks

00:13:15.842 --> 00:13:18.178
but would not be a part
of competition chess.

00:13:18.178 --> 00:13:20.764
We may play somebody
we don't like as much,

00:13:20.764 --> 00:13:21.973
or they don't like us.

00:13:21.973 --> 00:13:24.017
It's kind of life.

00:13:24.017 --> 00:13:27.729
And, yeah, sometimes, they
have or they've tried to

00:13:27.729 --> 00:13:31.691
psych you out and, you
know, get into your head,

00:13:31.691 --> 00:13:35.612
like, stare at you or bang a piece,

00:13:35.612 --> 00:13:38.073
make a move with a very loud movement,

00:13:38.073 --> 00:13:41.826
or something like that, or, you know.

00:13:41.826 --> 00:13:43.703
That, it's called bad sportsmanship.

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.874
Magneto: What do you do
when you wake up to that?

00:13:47.874 --> 00:13:50.210
Susan: So, psychology
can be very important.

00:13:50.210 --> 00:13:52.462
And it starts out right
from the very beginning.

00:13:52.462 --> 00:13:54.506
Because in the beginning of the game,

00:13:54.506 --> 00:13:56.800
there are many starting options,

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:01.012
and people have their favorite
options to start the game.

00:14:01.888 --> 00:14:04.724
And it's a little psychological board

00:14:04.724 --> 00:14:07.727
ongoing right from the
first move, whether,

00:14:07.727 --> 00:14:12.065
whose preparation will
be actually on the board?

00:14:12.065 --> 00:14:15.777
Who will walk into the other side's prep?

00:14:15.777 --> 00:14:18.905
Let's say they both
prepared for the same thing.

00:14:18.905 --> 00:14:20.657
Who got deeper into it?

00:14:20.657 --> 00:14:24.369
So that's a real interesting
psychological game

00:14:24.369 --> 00:14:25.620
right from the beginning.

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:27.747
And then, let's say they got out of that

00:14:27.747 --> 00:14:30.500
and nobody got the significant advantage,

00:14:31.751 --> 00:14:34.504
then later in the game, it's about,

00:14:34.504 --> 00:14:36.381
how do you feel about your position?

00:14:36.381 --> 00:14:38.842
In any of these movies and segments

00:14:38.842 --> 00:14:41.302
is that they intentionally or not

00:14:41.302 --> 00:14:43.888
don't show enough of the chess moves,

00:14:43.888 --> 00:14:45.181
unlike in "Queen's Gambit."

00:14:45.181 --> 00:14:48.476
[audience laughs]
[clock button clicking]

00:14:49.477 --> 00:14:51.688
You can clearly see something is wrong.

00:14:51.688 --> 00:14:54.190
If you zoom in, you can see that

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:56.735
in the corner there on
her right-hand side,

00:14:56.735 --> 00:14:58.194
it's a dark square.

00:14:58.194 --> 00:15:00.989
So that's when the board
is turned the wrong way,

00:15:00.989 --> 00:15:03.033
which is a very typical
mistake, by the way.

00:15:03.033 --> 00:15:06.745
There are so many commercials
and some movie scenes

00:15:06.745 --> 00:15:08.830
when they don't pay
attention to that detail.

00:15:08.830 --> 00:15:10.874
But also, the pieces are kind of

00:15:10.874 --> 00:15:13.126
really random on the board, as well.

00:15:13.126 --> 00:15:16.504
So this is the one that is thumbs down

00:15:16.504 --> 00:15:19.632
when it comes to the chess-technical part.

